%I A398821 #9 Aug 12 2026 01:36:52
%S A398821 44,48,49,63,75,80,98,99,116,147,171,175,207,244,260,275,315,324,332,
%T A398821 363,368,387,404,475,476,495,507,524,528,531,548,549,575,603,604,624,
%U A398821 636,656,692,724,725,747,764,774,819,832,844,845,846,847,867,891,908,924,931
%N A398821 Numbers k such that the powerful parts (A057521) of both k and k+1 are squares that are larger than 1.
%C A398821 Numbers k such that k and k+1 are both terms in A398820.
%C A398821 Subsequence of A342187 and differs from it by not having the terms 288, 360, 539, 675, 800, ... .
%C A398821 The asymptotic density of this sequence is d = Product_{p prime} (1 - 2/(p^2*(p+1))) - 2 * zeta(2) * Product_{p prime} (1 - 2/p^2 - 1/p^3 + 2/p^4) + Product_{p prime} (1 - 2/p^2) = 0.0573621036078291284166... . The number of terms not exceeding x is d * x + O(x^(2/3+eps)), where eps > 0 (Srichan et al., 2026, a consequence of Theorem 1.1).
%H A398821 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A398821/b398821.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A398821 Teerapat Srichan, Nithi Rungtanapirom, and Saeree Wananiyakul, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1051/ita/2026022">On the distribution of consecutive M-free integers</a>, RAIRO - Theoretical Informatics and Applications, Vol. 60 (2026), Article 22.
%e A398821 44 = 2^2 * 11 is a term since its powerful part, 4 = 2^2, is a square that is larger than 1, and the powerful part of 44 + 1 = 45 = 3^2 * 5 is 9 = 3^2, which is also a square that is larger than 1.
%t A398821 q[k_] := q[k] = Module[{e = FactorInteger[k][[;; , 2]]}, AnyTrue[e, # > 1 &] && AllTrue[e, # == 1 || EvenQ[#] &]]; Select[Range[950], q[#] && q[#+1] &]
%o A398821 (PARI) is1(k) = if(k == 1, 0, my(e = factor(k)[, 2]); vecmax(e) > 1 && #select(x -> (x > 1 && x%2), e) == 0);
%o A398821 isok(k) = is1(k) && is1(k+1);
%Y A398821 Subsequence of A342187 and A398820.
%Y A398821 Cf. A000290, A013661, A057521, A065474, A398161.
%K A398821 nonn,easy,new
%O A398821 1,1
